springboot.cicd

how you explain to interview question explain ci/cd pipeline in your project ,
where we are using jenkins with kubernetes in springboot application

1. Project Context:
“In my current project, we have a microservices-based architecture built with
Spring Boot, containerized using Docker, and deployed to a Kubernetes cluster
(EKS/GKE/AKS/on-prem).
We use Jenkins as our CI/CD orchestrator to automate the entire software
delivery process.”

2. CI/CD Flow Overview:
“Our CI/CD pipeline has the following stages, all orchestrated by
 Jenkins pipelines (written in a Jenkins file):”

3:
Code Commit
Developers push code to GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket.
A webhook triggers Jenkins.
Checkout Code
Jenkins checks out the latest code from the repo.
Build Stage
Jenkins runs mvn clean package to compile and package the Spring Boot app.
Unit & Integration Tests
Runs mvn test, fails the build if any test fails.
Test results published using Jenkins plugins.
Docker Image Build
Jenkins builds a Docker image of the app using a Dockerfile.
Push to Registry
The image is pushed to DockerHub, Amazon ECR, or a private registry.

4:CD (Continuous Deployment) – Deploy to Kubernetes
Kubernetes Deployment
Jenkins uses kubectl or Helm to apply updated Kubernetes manifests
(deployment.yaml, service.yaml, etc.).
The deployment pulls the latest image from the registry.
Rolling Updates
Kubernetes handles rolling updates to replace old pods with new ones without downtime.
Health Checks & Auto-Rollback
Readiness/liveness probes defined in Kubernetes detect unhealthy pods.
If a new deployment fails, Kubernetes can auto-revert to the previous version.

Post-deployment
Slack/Email Notifications are sent using Jenkins post-build actions.
Monitoring via Prometheus, Grafana, or ELK.
Logs and metrics are streamed to CloudWatch or Elasticsearch.

Real-World Example Summary
“So in short, every time code is committed:
Jenkins builds the app, tests it,
Creates a Docker image and pushes it to a registry,
Then updates the Kubernetes deployment with the new image.
This ensures that any code change is automatically tested and
deployed to our dev/staging/prod environments.”

| Area          | Bonus Points to Add                                                          |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Parallel jobs | “Tests and build stages run in parallel to speed up pipelines.”              |
| Environments  | “We use separate Jenkins jobs for dev, staging, and production deployments.” |
| Helm          | “We use Helm to manage complex Kubernetes templates.”                        |
| Rollbacks     | “We tag previous Docker images to allow easy rollback in case of failure.”   |


















build-qa:
    when:
      equal: [build-qa, << pipeline.parameters.action >>]
    jobs:
      - build:
          context: AWS
      - sonarqube/run-tests:
          context: AWS
          parallelism: 2
          executor: sonarqube/java21
      - sonarqube/analyze-without-running-tests:
          context: AWS
          executor: sonarqube/java21
          requires:
            - sonarqube/run-tests
      - test:
          context: AWS
      - deploy-qa:
          context: AWS
          requires:
            - build
            - test
            - sonarqube/analyze-without-running-tests
      - integration-test-qa:
          context: APP
          requires:
            - deploy-qa


This snippet is a CircleCI workflow configuration named build-qa.
It defines a CI/CD (Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment) pipeline
that automatically builds, tests, analyzes, and deploys your application to a
QA environment.
Here is a breakdown of exactly how it works:
1. Trigger Condition (when)
YAML


when:equal: [build-qa, << pipeline.parameters.action >>]

This workflow doesn't just run on every single code push. It uses a conditional clause.
It will only execute if a pipeline parameter named action is explicitly set to the
value "build-qa". This is common for manually triggered pipelines or webhooks.

2. The Jobs Pipeline (Order & Dependencies)
The jobs section outlines the tasks that run. Because CircleCI runs jobs in parallel
by default unless told otherwise, the requires key is used to map out the execution
order.

Here is how the jobs stack up and depend on each other:
Phase 1: Initial Tasks (Parallel)
The following jobs have no requires statement, meaning they all spin up
simultaneously as soon as the workflow starts:
build: Compiles or builds the application using AWS context credentials.
sonarqube/run-tests: Runs unit tests using a Java 21 executor.
 The parallelism:2 flag means CircleCI will split and run these tests across 2
 separate parallel containers to speed things up.
test: Likely runs another suite of tests (like quick linting or integration smoke tests) under the AWS context.
Phase 2: Code Analysis
sonarqube/analyze-without-running-tests: This job handles sending the code quality
and coverage results to SonarQube.
Dependency: It requires sonarqube/run-tests to finish first so it can use the
test reports generated in Phase 1.
Phase 3: Deployment
deploy-qa: Deploys the built application to the QA environment.
Dependency: It acts as a gatekeeper. It will only run if build, test,
and sonarqube/analyze-without-running-tests all complete successfully.
If your tests fail or the build breaks, it won't deploy.
Phase 4: Post-Deployment Testing
integration-test-qa: Runs integration tests against the newly deployed QA environment.
Dependency: It requires deploy-qa to finish successfully.
(You can't test a deployment that hasn't happened yet!)
Note that this job switches from the AWS security context to an APP context.
Visualizing the Workflow

[ build ] ─────────────────────────┐
                                   │
[ sonarqube/run-tests ]            ▼
         │                  [ deploy-qa ] ──► [ integration-test-qa ]
         ▼                         ▲
[ sonarqube/analyze... ] ──────────┤
                                   │
[ test ] ──────────────────────────┘

Key Takeaway
What you have here is a robust, well-orchestrated QA pipeline.
It aggressively runs builds and tests in parallel early on to save time,
gates the deployment behind a wall of code-quality and test checks,
and then automatically verifies the deployment with integration tests.

Key Docker Concepts to Know:
    Dockerfile: A simple text file containing a list of commands and instructions that
    Docker uses to build an image. It’s like a recipe for your application environment.
    Docker Image: A read-only blueprint or snapshot of your containerized application.
    It contains the code, libraries, and dependencies. You can share these images on
    registries like Docker Hub.
    Docker Container: A live, running instance of a Docker Image.
    If the image is the blueprint, the container is the actual house built from that
    blueprint.

dockerfile:
# 1. Use an official Node.js runtime as a base image
FROM node:20

# 2. Set the working directory inside the container
WORKDIR /app

# 3. Copy dependencies and install them
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install

# 4. Copy the rest of the application code
COPY . .

# 5. Expose the port the app runs on
EXPOSE 3000

# 6. Define the command to run the app
CMD ["node", "server.js"]